<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of Jenkins' Ear</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1748">1748</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of Jenkins' Ear was a conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> fought mainly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and on the American mainland from 1739. It took its name from the ear of Robert Jenkins, a British captain mutilated by Spanish coastguards in 1731. After a British failure at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> in 1741, it merged into the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> and ended with the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1748.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear.jpg" alt="War of Jenkins' Ear" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Overton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_new_and_correct_map_of_the_trading_part_of_the_West_Indies.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a> of 1713 gave British merchants access to Spanish American markets through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asiento">Asiento</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a>, a monopoly to supply 5,000 slaves a year, and the Navío de Permiso, which allowed two ships a year to sell goods at Porto Bello and Veracruz. Both were held by the South Sea Company. Smuggling was too profitable to stop, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> tried either to manage the illegal trade or to use it as an instrument of policy.</p><p>The war is often portrayed as a trade dispute, but modern historians argue that Spanish concern over British colonial expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> mattered equally. The Tory opposition, seeking to unseat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a>'s Whig government, also campaigned for war. In April 1731 the Spanish guarda costa captain was alleged to have severed the ear of the British captain Robert Jenkins, and in 1738 opposition politicians used the incident to rouse support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-vernon">Edward Vernon</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> on 22 November 1739 with six ships of the line. Its fall was greeted with enthusiasm in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and Rule Britannia was written in 1740 to mark it. Vernon then tried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a>, the Spanish gold-trading port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, three times. His largest attack, with an amphibious force from 13 March to 20 May 1741, failed against the fortifications and the strategy of the Spanish commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blas-de-lezo">Blas de Lezo</a>. Heavy British losses came mostly from disease, chiefly yellow fever, which took more lives than the fighting.</p><p>Commodore George Anson sailed for the Pacific in September 1740. Most of his men died of disease, but on 20 June 1743 he captured a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> carrying more than a million gold coins and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> having circled the globe. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a> besieged St. Augustine in 1740 without success, and in 1742 he defeated a Spanish landing from 2,000 troops at Bloody Marsh. Privateers on both sides were active, and Spanish ones seized hundreds of British ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>By mid-1742 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> had broken out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its scale drew attention away from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Vernon's return that year ended major offensive operations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> entered the war in 1744, and fear of a French invasion persuaded British policymakers against sending large forces overseas. Fighting formally ended with the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1748, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> largely failed to achieve its territorial and economic aims in the Americas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of Jenkins' Ear” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>War of Jenkins' Ear</b> (1739–1748) a British attack on St. Augustine and a Spanish invasion of Georgia were both repulsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain did not yet rule the seas; written during the <b>War of Jenkins' Ear</b>, it may have answered alleged Spanish attacks on British merchant ships.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a></li><li><blockquote>James Oglethorpe, founder of the colony of Georgia, began a British attack on St Augustine in Spanish Florida, part of the <b>War of Jenkins' Ear</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-13">13 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>War of Jenkins' Ear</b> Britain sent Robert Hodgson to the coast, and in 1749 a superintendency was formally established, with its headquarters at Black River on the Honduran coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/mosquito-shore">Mosquito Shore</a></li></ul></section>
